Subject: fonts: serve page-visible font enumeration from the profile

The compositor has selected and merged fonts.enumeration_allowlist since patch
0081, and until this patch nothing read it. The loader did not parse the field,
no emitter consulted it, and every route a page has for asking which fonts are
installed answered from the host. Measured on the shipped macos-arm64 artifact
with a profile carrying the macOS core font pack as its allowlist: Futura,
Didot, Baskerville, Apple Chancery, Hoefler Text and Avenir Next are all
installed on the host, none of them is in the allowlist, and all six were
reported present by document.fonts.check(), by canvas measureText, by CSS
layout width and by queryLocalFonts(), which returned all 536 faces and 184
families the host has. The claim was described in the profile and contradicted
by the browser.

The filter is subtractive and can only be subtractive. A family the host does
not have cannot be made to measure correctly, so the allowlist removes families
this host has and the claimed machine does not; it cannot add. Adding needs the
font files, which is provisioning, and provisioning is a separate problem that
this patch does not pretend to solve.

Blink has no single function that every probe passes through, so the work is to
find the smallest set of places where a family name the page wrote is handed to
the platform, and to route all of them through one predicate so they cannot
disagree. A filter on document.fonts.check that does not also apply to canvas
measurement is worse than none, because the two disagreeing is a signal no real
machine produces. The predicate is Profile::FontFamilyEnumerable, it lives in
base next to the parsed profile, and the browser process and every renderer ask
the same function.

There are four such places.

FontFallbackList::GetFontData holds the two calls that hand a page-written
family name to FontCache. Everything that measures or renders text by name goes
through it: canvas measureText, CSS layout, SVG text, and the worker and
OffscreenCanvas paths, because a Font object owns a FontFallbackList whatever
created it. A hidden family takes the branch an absent family already takes, so
the next family in the list is tried and the page is not left without a font.

CSSFontSelectorBase::IsPlatformFamilyMatchAvailable is what
document.fonts.check() consults. check() is not the blunt instrument it looks
like: FontFaceSet::check returns true for any family the platform can match,
whatever the page's own @font-face rules say, so a page that declares an unused
@font-face for a family name and then calls check() on it reads platform
availability directly. That probe now reads the same availability the
measurement path does.

LocalFontFaceSource covers @font-face { src: local(...) }, which matches a
PostScript or full font name rather than a family name. The allowlist cannot be
consulted with the string as the page wrote it, because "ArialMT" is Arial and
no list of families would spell it that way, so the face is resolved and the
family the platform actually produced is what decides. IsLocalFontAvailable and
CreateFontData both check, because a rule that reports unavailable and then
renders is exactly the disagreement a probe is looking for.

FontEnumerationCache::BuildEnumerationCache is the browser-side table behind
queryLocalFonts(). It is the only place that table is built and it is
platform-independent, so one removal there covers macOS, Windows and Linux.
Nothing is ever added to it: an invented entry would carry a postscript name
with no typeface behind it, and the next thing a page does with a FontData is
measure it or call blob().

Four things are deliberately left resolving.

Generic families are exempt, and this is load-bearing rather than cautious.
serif, sans-serif, monospace, cursive, fantasy, system-ui and math are not host
families; they are a browser setting that the font selector resolves through
FamilyNameFromSettings into a concrete family, and that concrete family is
often not in a claimed machine's installed set. Measured on the reference host,
cursive resolves to Apple Chancery, which the macOS core pack does not list.
Filtering the resolved name would leave ordinary pages with no cursive font,
and filtering on a page's own spelling of a generic name would break every page
on the web.

Character fallback is exempt. FontCache::FallbackFontForCharacter and
GetLastResortFallbackFont do not take a page-supplied name; they ask the
platform for something that can draw a codepoint, which on macOS reaches Apple
Color Emoji for emoji and Times or Lucida Grande for the last resort. Filtering
there would produce tofu, which breaks pages visibly and is trivially
detectable.

Web fonts are untouched without doing anything, because a page's @font-face is
answered from the font face cache before the host is ever asked. The one
interesting case is a page declaring @font-face for a family name the profile
hides; the page's own font wins, which is correct, and that is what a real
machine does too.

The families a claimed platform cannot be without remain present whenever the
host has them, because the core pack is unioned into the allowlist by the
compositor. When the host does not have them, this patch cannot help: the
compositor already reports that as a limitation, and no filter can conjure a
typeface.

What remains open, and it is not small. The filter faithfully serves whatever
set the profile states, so the quality of the claim is now the quality of that
list. The checked-in macOS core pack holds 32 families because the collector
probed a fixed 76-name list, while the same host enumerates 184 families
through queryLocalFonts. Enforcing the current pack therefore presents a macOS
machine with 30 visible families where a real one has 184, and that is its own
tell under queryLocalFonts, which needs a permission prompt but is not exotic.
The mechanism is right and the data is a subset; widening the core pack to a
measured complete family set is a catalogue change, not a code change, and this
patch should land with it rather than ahead of it.

Compile-unverified: this cannot be built in this session.

